{% extends "base.twig" %}

{%  block title %}Showcase authentication{% endblock %}

{% block content %}

<h1>Showcase / Campus authentication</h1>

<div class="alert alert-success">Hey! A few libraries are available in PHP (from <a href="https://github.com/doyoubuzz/dyb-api-connexion-php/tree/master/Showcase">DoYouBuzz</a> and <a href="https://github.com/AppVentus/DybApiBundle">AppVentus</a>) and in <a href="https://github.com/MrRuru/doyoubuzz-showcase">Ruby</a>.</div>

<p>If you use DoYouBuzz Showcase or DoYouBuzz Campus, you can access the datas of your application, users and CV with this this API.</p>

<h2>API keys</h2>

<p>API keys are managed by an administrator of your Showcase / Campus account, under <strong>Settings</strong>, then <strong>API</strong>. From that page you can create as many keys as you need, and revoke them at any time.</p>

<p>Each key is made of two values:</p>

<table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
	<tr>
		<th>Value</th>
		<th>Description</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>Public key</td>
		<td>Looks like <code>shw_live_3f9a1c74be2058d6a1f4c39e7b0d82f4</code>. This is the value you send as the <code>apikey</code> parameter of every request. It stays visible in the settings page.</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>Secret</td>
		<td>A 64 characters hexadecimal string, used to sign your requests. <strong>It is displayed only once, right after the creation of the key</strong>, and can never be read again: store it in your application configuration immediately. If you lose it, revoke the key and create a new one.</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<p>When creating a key you choose its lifetime: unlimited, 30, 90 or 365 days. An expired or revoked key is refused exactly like an unknown one.</p>

<p>The <strong>API consumption</strong> page of the same settings section shows your traffic, your <a href="/pro/rate-limit">rate limits</a> and the calls that have been rejected, per key and per endpoint.</p>

<div class="alert alert-info">Keys created before the introduction of the key manager still work, but they use the historical signature scheme described in the <a href="#legacy">Legacy keys</a> section at the bottom of this page. Every key you create today uses the HMAC signature described just below.</div>

<h2>How to access protected resources</h2>

<p><span class="label label-info">GET | PUT | POST | DELETE</span> <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/&lt;method&gt;?apikey=:apikey&amp;timestamp=:timestamp&amp;nonce=:nonce&amp;hash=:hash</code></p>

<h3>Mandatory parameters</h3>

<p>There are a few mandatory parameters for each request made to the Showcase API :</p>

<table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
	<tr>
		<th>Parameter</th>
		<th>Description</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>apikey (mandatory)</td>
		<td>
			The public key of your application (<code>shw_live_…</code>), obtained in the "Settings", then "API" section of your DoYouBuzz Showcase account.
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>timestamp (mandatory)</td>
		<td>
			The Unix timestamp of your request, <strong>in seconds</strong> (10 digits). Your request is accepted only if this timestamp is less than <strong>5 minutes</strong> (300 seconds) away from the server time, in the past or in the future.
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>nonce (mandatory)</td>
		<td>
			A random value, <strong>unique for each request</strong> (a UUID or a few random hexadecimal bytes are perfect). Never reuse one: replaying an already signed URL is rejected.
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>hash (mandatory)</td>
		<td>
			The signature of your request, that you must compute and send for each call. A detailed explanation with a code example is given below.
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<div class="alert">
	<strong>Watch out for milliseconds.</strong> Several languages (JavaScript, Java...) give you a timestamp in milliseconds. A 13 digits timestamp is <strong>always rejected</strong>: divide it by 1000 and send the integer part.
</div>

<h4>How to compute the hash parameter?</h4>

<p>The hash is an HMAC SHA-256 of a canonical representation of your request, keyed with your secret. Because the HTTP method and the path are part of what you sign, a signed URL cannot be replayed on another endpoint.</p>

<h5>In plain english</h5>

<ol>
	<li>Build the <em>canonical string</em>: three lines joined by a line feed (<code>\n</code>).
		<ol>
			<li>The HTTP method, <strong>in upper case</strong>: <code>GET</code>, <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code> or <code>DELETE</code>.</li>
			<li>The path of the request, <strong>and nothing else</strong>: no scheme, no host, no query string. For <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/users/666?apikey=…</code> the path is <code>/api/v1/users/666</code>.</li>
			<li>The query parameters: take all the parameters you send in the URL (both mandatory and optional) <strong>except <code>hash</code></strong>, sort them by parameter name, encode both the name and the value with a RFC 3986 encoding (<code>rawurlencode()</code> in PHP, <code>encodeURIComponent()</code> in JavaScript), write them as <code>name=value</code> and join them with <code>&amp;</code>. A parameter holding an array must be JSON encoded before being URL encoded.</li>
		</ol>
	</li>
	<li>Compute the HMAC SHA-256 of that string, using your <strong>secret</strong> as the key, and send the hexadecimal digest as the <code>hash</code> parameter.</li>
</ol>

<h5>PHP function</h5>

<p>This function takes the HTTP method, the path of the request, an associative array of the parameters to send, and the secret of your key. It returns the parameters with the computed <code>hash</code> added :</p>

<pre name="code" class="php">
function computeHash($method, $pathInfo, $params, $apiSecret)
{
    unset($params['hash']);
    ksort($params);

    $pairs = array();
    foreach ($params as $paramKey => $paramVal) {
        if (is_array($paramVal)) {
            $paramVal = json_encode($paramVal);
        }
        $pairs[] = rawurlencode($paramKey) . '=' . rawurlencode($paramVal);
    }

    $canonical = strtoupper($method) . "\n" . $pathInfo . "\n" . implode('&', $pairs);

    return array_merge($params, array('hash' => hash_hmac('sha256', $canonical, $apiSecret)));
}
</pre>

<h5>Example</h5>

<p>Let's say you want to get the user with the id 666. According to the documentation you need to send a <span class="label label-info">GET</span> to <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/users/666</code> with these parameters :</p>

<table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
	<tr>
		<th>Parameter name</th>
		<th>Parameter value</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>apikey</td>
		<td><code>shw_live_3f9a1c74be2058d6a1f4c39e7b0d82f4</code></td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>timestamp</td>
		<td><code>1774267200</code></td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>nonce</td>
		<td><code>7b1f4c9a2e8d6035</code></td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>hash</td>
		<td>???</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<p>So, let's compute the hash!</p>

<ol>
	<li>First, build the canonical string. The method is <code>GET</code>, the path is <code>/api/v1/users/666</code>, and the parameters sorted by name give <code>apikey=shw_live_3f9a1c74be2058d6a1f4c39e7b0d82f4&amp;nonce=7b1f4c9a2e8d6035&amp;timestamp=1774267200</code> (none of these values needs to be escaped). Joined by line feeds, the canonical string is :

<pre>GET
/api/v1/users/666
apikey=shw_live_3f9a1c74be2058d6a1f4c39e7b0d82f4&amp;nonce=7b1f4c9a2e8d6035&amp;timestamp=1774267200</pre>
	</li>
	<li>Then compute the HMAC SHA-256 of that string with your secret as the key. Here the secret is <code>c4e1a9f7b2d85630fa47e91c8b0d2356e7f1a4c980b3d62e5f8a17c4b9d0e236</code>, and you get your hash parameter : <code>be49e8c3daffdfe07b90fd1ee47f3062960f82bba4238b2ec244fbbacc63d621</code></li>
</ol>

<p>Now, you can send the request to the API : </p>

<p><span class="label label-info">GET</span> <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/users/666?apikey=shw_live_3f9a1c74be2058d6a1f4c39e7b0d82f4&amp;timestamp=1774267200&amp;nonce=7b1f4c9a2e8d6035&amp;hash=be49e8c3daffdfe07b90fd1ee47f3062960f82bba4238b2ec244fbbacc63d621</code></p>

<h3>Authentication errors</h3>

<p>Every authentication problem is answered with a <span class="label label-important">403</span>, whatever its cause: missing parameter, unknown / revoked / expired key, timestamp out of the 5 minutes window, wrong signature, or reused nonce.</p>

<p>For security reasons, an unknown key, a revoked key and an expired key all share the very same message ("This api key is not valid!"), so you cannot tell them apart from the response. If you get a 403 while your signature code is known to work, check the state of your key in the settings page.</p>

<p>Note that the body of a 403 is a plain error response, it does not use the JSON envelope of the 429 described in the <a href="/pro/rate-limit">rate limiting</a> page.</p>

<h2 id="legacy">Legacy keys</h2>

<p>This section only concerns the API keys created <strong>before</strong> the introduction of the key manager. They keep working, unchanged, with the historical signature scheme: no <code>nonce</code>, and a MD5 signature. If you are integrating the API today, or if you created your key from the "API" settings page, use the HMAC scheme described above instead.</p>

<h4>How to compute the hash parameter?</h4>

<h5>In plain english function</h5>
<ol>
	  <li>Take all the parameters sent in the URL (both mandatory and optionals), order them by alphabetical order (according to their name), then concatenate their value.</li>
	  <li>Add the apiSecret at the end of the resulting string</li>
	  <li>MD5 the result: this is your hash</li>
</ol>

<h5>PHP Function</h5>


<p>This function takes two parameters : an associative arrays of the parameters to send, and the apiSecret :</p>

<pre name="code" class="php">
function computeHash($params, $apiSecret)
{
    $paramsStr = "";
    ksort($params);
    foreach ($params as $paramKey => $paramVal) {
        $paramsStr .= $paramVal;
    }
    $hash = md5($paramsStr . $apiSecret);
    return array_merge($params, array('hash' => $hash));
}
</pre>


<h5>Example</h5>

<p>Let's say you want to get the CV with the id 16546 (you have previously verified you can access this ressource). To do so, according to the documentation you need to send a <span class="label label-info">GET</span> to <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/dyb/cv/16546</code> with these parameters :</p>

<table class="table table-bordered table-striped">
	<tr>
		<th>Parameter name</th>
		<th>Parameter value</th>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>timestamp</td>
		<td>1359021513</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>apikey</td>
		<td>
			IuQSDLKQLSDK344590Li987
		</td>
	</tr>
	<tr>
		<td>hash</td>
		<td>
			???
		</td>
	</tr>
</table>

<p>So, let's compute the hash!</p>

<ol>
	<li>First, order the parameters according to their name, then concatenate their value. You will have the following string : <code>IuQSDLKQLSDK344590Li9871359021513</code>.</li>
	<li>Then, append your apiSecret at the end of this string (here the apiSecret is <code>IuJyt42BnUiOlPM8FvB67tG</code>. You will have the resulting string : <code>IuQSDLKQLSDK344590Li9871359021513IuJyt42BnUiOlPM8FvB67tG</code>.</li>
	<li>Use this string to create a MD5, and you will have your hash parameter : <code>44f36e1dd04a10c0be21b2d8e880f22b</code></li>
</ol>

<p>Now, you can send the request to the API : </p>

<p><span class="label label-info">GET</span> <code>https://showcase.doyoubuzz.com/api/v1/dyb/cv/16546?apikey=IuQSDLKQLSDK344590Li987&amp;timestamp=1359021513&hash=44f36e1dd04a10c0be21b2d8e880f22b</code></p>

<p>&nbsp;</p>






